# SABOTAGE: T163 / ADR-051 R4 — the tally rebuild stops RECOMPUTING: its upsert no longer
# replaces `quantity` on an existing tally, so the first rebuild's total sticks forever.
# Late captures land in the ledger and never reach the tally, and so never reach the
# provider — usage that happened silently goes unbilled. (The mirror image of the
# additive bug: a rebuild that ADDS double-counts; one that never updates under-counts.)
#
# Flips the late-capture test. The first-rebuild derivation test and the idempotency
# test stay green (a fresh tally is still inserted with the right total, and re-running
# is still a no-op), which is exactly why this regression would pass a shallow suite.
# APP: demo
# TEST_FILES: test/usage_tally_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: a late capture grows the tally on the next rebuild; what was reported is untouched
diff --git a/samen_core/lib/samen/billing/usage_tally.ex b/samen_core/lib/samen/billing/usage_tally.ex
index ccb07f8..7c3f77d 100644
--- a/samen_core/lib/samen/billing/usage_tally.ex
+++ b/samen_core/lib/samen/billing/usage_tally.ex
@@ -96,7 +96,8 @@ defmodule Samen.Billing.UsageTally do
       upsert_identity: :unique_tally,
       # REPLACE the total (recompute), never add to it. `reported_quantity` is not
       # listed, so a rebuild never disturbs what has already been reported.
-      upsert_fields: [:quantity, :period_end],
+      # SABOTAGE (ADR-051 R4): an existing tally is never recomputed.
+      upsert_fields: [],
       authorize?: false
     )
     |> Ash.Changeset.set_context(%{private: %{Guard.marker_key() => true}})
